BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fall Ballet
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261003
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261004
UID:865da563239a127f5b2025c6bfd7e1e0@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:White Shadows\nChristian Claessens\nLongtime Jacobs faculty me
 mber Christian Claessens teams up with the Jacobs Percussion Department to
  create this world premiere based on the last three movements of Steve Rei
 ch’s Sextet\, employing marimbas\, vibraphones\, bass drums\, and more. 
 The eight dancers embody movement from pulsations to melodic patterns to r
 hythmic ambiguity.\nChristian Claessens\, Choreographer\nJoseph Gramley\, 
 Conductor and Chamber Music Coach\nBlake Kile\, Lighting Designer\nValse-F
 antaisie\nGeorge Balanchine\nAttended by their single partner\, five balle
 rinas move in a whirl of perpetual motion to Mikhail Glinka’s waltzing r
 hythms. Although it was popularly called “the Melancholy Waltz\,” this
  1967 rendering is fast and light and was originally presented as the seco
 nd section of Glinkiana\, which featured former IU Distinguished Professor
  Violette Verdy in its New York City Ballet premiere.\nGeorge Balanchine\,
  Choreographer\nKyra Nichols\, Stager\nBlake Kile\, Lighting Designer\nDig
 nity in Restraint\nCaili Quan\nBorn to a Chamorro Filipino family in Guam\
 , Caili Quan created this punchy work on New York City Ballet dancers to B
 eethoven’s String Quartet No. 12. Her love for “showing people the mus
 ic” is on full display as she corresponds five major chords to the five 
 core emotions of anger\, fear\, disgust\, joy\, and sadness.\nCaili Quan\,
  Choreographer\nAndrea Yorita\, Stager\nBlake Kile\, Lighting Designer\nWo
 rld Premiere\nJames Whiteside\nA principal dancer and choreographer with A
 merican Ballet Theatre\, James Whiteside is also a pop music performer\, m
 odel\, drag queen\, podcast host\, and author. He brings his wildly eclect
 ic style to Jacobs for several weeks as he creates his latest work\, with 
 the students of the Ballet Department.\nJames Whiteside\, Choreographer\nM
 ichael Breeden\, Stager\nBlake Kile\, Lighting Designer
GEO:39.1665575;-86.51751229999999
LOCATION:Musical Arts Center\, 101 N Eagleson Ave\, Bloomington\, IN 47406
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/fall-ballet/59542/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Grass: A Nation's Battle for Life w/ Q&A | IU Cinema Jon Vickers S
 coring Award
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261003T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261003T204500
UID:10870505779b315ad4842dd20bb6c967@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:In 1924\, neophyte filmmakers Merian C. Cooper and Ernest B. S
 choedsack\, who would later co-direct King Kong (1933)\, hooked up with jo
 urnalist and sometime-spy Marguerite Harrison and set off to film an adven
 ture. They found excitement\, danger\, and unparalleled drama in the migra
 tion of the Bakhtiari tribe of Persia (now Iran). Twice a year\, more than
  50\,000 people and half a million animals surmounted seemingly impossible
  obstacles to take their herds to pasture. The filmmakers captured unforge
 ttable images of courage and determination as the Bakhtiari braved the rag
 ing and icy waters of the half-mile-wide Karun River. Although many docume
 ntary historians consider Grass second only to Nanook of the North\, few p
 eople have actually ever seen this legendary film. This restored and full-
 length version will astonish today’s audiences with its beautiful photog
 raphy and heart-stopping adventure. Presented with a recorded orchestral s
 core by Patrick Holcomb that was world-premiered in IU Cinema's Virtual Sc
 reening Room in 2021. [71 min\; documentary\; silent film with intertitles
 ]\nCelebrating its first decade in 2026\, the IU Cinema Vickers Scoring Aw
 ard is named after IU Cinema’s founding director Jon Vickers\; it is an 
 IU Cinema-exclusive program produced in partnership with the Jacobs School
  of Music\, and is generously endowed by former IU Trustee the Honorable P
 .A. Mack Jr.\nA Q&A with composer Patrick Holcomb and IU Jacobs School of 
 Music Professor Larry Groupé\, moderated by IU Cinema Director Emeritus J
 on Vickers\, will follow the screening.
GEO:39.168585;-86.517361
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 E. 7th Street\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/grass-a-nations-battle-for-life
 -w-qa-iu-cinema-jon-vickers-scoring-award/59838/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
