BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Treasures of the Silk Road
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261021T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261021T180000
UID:6795402f85417f74a3a9258a52dbad0f@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:The McCalla Center\, in collaboration with musician and schola
 r Shahyar Daneshgar\, celebrates the opening of its new exhibition Treasur
 es of the Silk Road on Friday\, September 25th\, 4:00 pm-6:00 pm EDT.\nThe
  exhibit features over 70 musical instruments from the Pastel-Daneshgar mu
 sical instrument collection\, with a focus on strings\, flutes\, and percu
 ssion from across Asia\, the Middle East\, and North Africa. Crafted from 
 wood\, snakeskin\, horsehair\, lambskin\, camel bone\, and other unique ma
 terials\, these instruments provide a compelling history of cultural and a
 rtistic exchanges that have led to the myriad musical sounds and styles th
 at permeate the world today. The opening event will feature remarks from t
 he collection owner\, Shahyar Daneshgar\, and will feature musical clips p
 rovided by the Lotus World Music & Arts Festival.\nShahyar Daneshgar is a 
 lifelong musician\, scholar\, and cultural ambassador whose passion for tr
 aditional music has shaped more than three decades of collecting\, teachin
 g\, and performing. Born into a large musical family\, he studied at the T
 ehran Conservatory of Music before pursuing academic work in Germany and l
 ater at Indiana University\, where he earned his Ph.D. in Central Eurasian
  Studies in 1995. He taught at Indiana University until his retirement in 
 2024. He is founder and director of the Bloomington Silk Road Ensemble.\nT
 he exhibition is free and runs from September 25\, 2026\, to September 202
 7.\nThe mission of the McCalla Center is to provide accessible exhibition 
 and educational experiences\, creating opportunities for students\, facult
 y\, and the community to engage with collections\, IU-generated creative a
 ctivity\, and community projects in meaningful and innovative ways.
GEO:39.1709246;-86.527549
LOCATION:McCalla Center\, McCalla Center\, 525 E. 9th Street\, Bloomington
 \, IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/treasures-of-the-silk-road/5960
 4/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Yonder Mountain String Band
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261021T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261021T200000
UID:5cdde70c9c27a6ea3eca5d6fe2c4e452@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:Grammy-nominated Yonder Mountain String Band set the tone for 
 a new way forward in acoustic music\, carrying bluegrass into rooms and co
 nversations it had never reached before.\nNearly three decades later\, tha
 t same spirit still guides them\, alive and present on Good As True (2026)
 \, the band’s 12th studio album\, arriving March 27th\, 2026. The record
  captures Yonder in full stride. It’s unguarded and in motion\, preservi
 ng the spark of musicians playing in real time and leaning into everything
  they have learned along the way.\nGood As True (2026) digs into communica
 tion\, the conversations that carry us forward and the ones that fall apar
 t. The things we say\, the things we mean\, and what gets lost in between.
 \n“Brand New Heartache\,” the album’s lead single\, lives in that fr
 agile moment after a breakup when heartbreak and forward motion exist at t
 he same time. Built on rock-leaning verses and a bluegrass-lifted chorus\,
  it turns loss into motion and sets the tone for the record. From the inde
 lible guitar riff and self-reckoning at the heart of “Blind” to the sh
 arp\, sarcastic truth of “Long Ride\,” the album stays rooted in real 
 life\, tracing personal and political fractures on “Nothing New” and 
 “The Lie.” “One to One Another” and “Always Almost” linger in 
 the struggle to connect.\n“Barroom Feather” stands on its own. Recorde
 d live in the studio\, the song began with lyrics and drifted into a spont
 aneous jam that became one of the record’s most expansive moments\, stre
 tching past sixteen minutes. Anchored by a subtle drum track\, it broadens
  the band’s rhythmic range without losing Yonder’s acoustic core.\nAcr
 oss eight original tracks co-written by Adam Aijala (guitar\, vocals)\, Da
 ve Johnston (banjo\, vocals)\, Ben Kaufmann (bass vocals)\, and Nick Picci
 ninni (mandolin\, vocals)\, Good As True (2026) reflects a group writing f
 rom inside their own history\, relationships\, and the world around them. 
 Brought to life by the full five-piece lineup\, including fiddle player Co
 leman Smith\, the album carries the interplay and identity of the band as 
 it exists today. The songs move between drive\, reflection\, humor\, and w
 eight. It is forward-looking and fully alive\, a reminder that Yonder Moun
 tain String Band’s story is still unfolding.
GEO:39.1662844;-86.5328085
LOCATION:Buskirk-Chumley Theater\, 114 E. Kirkwood Avenue\, Bloomington\, 
 IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/yonder-mountain-string-band/592
 18/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
