BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:An Evening With Wright Thompson and Michael Koryta
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261025T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261025T180000
UID:80bdf30ba0a2fda117f87ff62e6fee65@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:BLOOMINGTON BOOK FEST: WRIGHT THOMPSON\nJoin the Bloomington B
 ook Festival for the third annual presentation of the Bob Hammel Writing A
 ward.\nThe 2026 winner and keynote speaker is Wright Thompson\, a New York
  Times-bestselling author and revered journalist. A native of Clarksdale\,
  in the Mississippi Delta\, Thompson has spent two decades proving that th
 e best stories are often about ambition\, loss\, family\, and the price of
  greatness.\nHis latest book\, The Barn: The Secret History of a Murder in
  Mississippi\, is a haunting account of the brutal 1955 murder of Emmett T
 ill\, named a Best Book of the Year by the Washington Post\, TIME\, and Va
 nity Fair\, and nominated for a PEN America Literary Award.\nIn his journa
 lism for ESPN and The Atlantic\, Thompson has writen the definitive profil
 es of legends ranging from Ted Williams to Michael Jordan\, revealed the s
 ecret history of Tiger Woods’ Navy SEAL fascination\, and penned many of
  the most-read articles in ESPN’s history. Some of those pieces were col
 lected in his New York Times bestseller\, The Cost of These Dreams: Sports
  Stories and Other Serious Business.\nAnother bestseller was Pappyland: A 
 Story of Family\, Fine Bourbon\, and the Things That Last\, which is both 
 a chronicle of the remarkable history of world-famous Pappy Van Winkle whi
 skey and a meditation on the traditions worth preserving.\nWe’re hoping 
 Wright will offer an opinion on Indiana’s finest bourbon by the end of t
 he night\, and we can promise a few rare bottles will be on the auction ta
 ble.\nPresenting the Hammel Award and joining Wright in conversation is Bl
 oomington native Michael Koryta\, a New York Times-bestselling author\, wh
 ose new novel\, Die Famous\, releases September 22. Set in Indiana in 1937
 \, the novel follows Savannah Cody and her son Alford as they pursue the m
 an she once loved and the nation now fears – the outlaw Nickel Terhune 
 – from the Midwest to Maine. Stephen King called Die Famous “the best 
 gangster novel I’ve ever read.” The evening with Wright Thompson will 
 be Koryta’s only appearance in Bloomington this fall. Both writers will 
 sign books following the event.\nDon’t miss the chance to hear these aut
 hors talk about their craft\, influences\, – and bourbon preferences! Wh
 ile also raising money for the good work of BloomREADS.
GEO:39.1662844;-86.5328085
LOCATION:Buskirk-Chumley Theater\, 114 E. Kirkwood Avenue\, Bloomington\, 
 IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/an-evening-with-wright-thompson
 -and-michael-koryta/59219/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Kagemusha | Michael A. McRobbie's Choice
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261025T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261025T214500
UID:4840d243ee681f74053c3963f53f31c5@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:Akira Kurosawa's lauded feudal epic follows the story of a pet
 ty thief recruited to impersonate an aging warlord in an effort to keep th
 e warlord safe from attacking competing clans. When the warlord dies\, his
  general reluctantly empowers the imposter to take over as the ruler to ma
 intain their control. But his commitment to his new role is tested when fo
 rced to lead “his” troops into battle. Winner of the 1980 Cannes Film 
 Festival Palme d’Or\, Kagemusha merges the passion of youthful artistic 
 creation with the genius of a master filmmaker. [162 min\; drama\, war\; J
 apanese with English subtitles]
GEO:39.168585;-86.517361
LOCATION:IU Cinema\, 1213 E. 7th Street\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
